          "file": ".github/workflows/review.yml",
          "lineStart": 43,
          "lineEnd": 59,
          "title": "Self-review bypass: PR head becomes the trusted reviewer",
          "whyItBreaks": "The old workflow checked out the PR head as an untrusted `target/` and separately checked out the reviewer from `frankekn/needlefish@main` into `needlefish/`, then ran the trusted tool path `$GITHUB_WORKSPACE/needlefish/src/cli.ts`. This diff deletes that second trusted checkout and both `working-directory` settings, so the review step now runs `./node_modules/.bin/tsx src/cli.ts` and `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` from the PR head itself. A PR that edits `src/cli.ts` controls the program that produces its own review verdict; because the job grants `pull-requests: write` and `checks: write` (lines 16-19) and passes `GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}` (line 56), that program can self-approve, forge a passing/blocking verdict, or exfiltrate the token. The `pull_request` condition (line 24) only limits forks, and `workflow_dispatch` (line 24 first clause) bypasses it entirely, so a manually dispatched review of a fork PR also executes that fork's `src/cli.ts` and install scripts on the self-hosted runner. The base commit's own comment explicitly identified this threat ('This prevents a PR from controlling its own review by modifying the tool'), which the new comment removes."
        }
      ],
